Since the thermal emission of debris disks at millimeter wavelengths is optically thin, the dust mass can be estimated as $$\begin{equation}
M_\mathrm{dust} = \frac{{F_\mathrm{1.33mm}} d^2}{ B_{\nu}(T_\mathrm{BB}) \kappa_{\nu}}, \label{eq:mdust}
\end{equation}$$

Taking an optically thin $J=2-1$ rotational transition line of the observed isotopologs, $^{12}$CO, $^{13}$CO, or C$^{18}$O, the total mass of $^{12}$CO gas can be estimated as: $$\begin{equation}
M_{CO} = {4 \pi m d^2} \frac{F_\mathrm{21}}{x_2 h \nu_\mathrm{21} A_\mathrm{21}} f_{^{12}C^{16}O/^{x}C^{y}O},    
\end{equation}$$
